We have class RoleList extends ArrayList<Object>. Method set(int index, Role role). Preconditions: index valid? The comment says: "If the index is valid, within the list's bounds, and the role is not null, then for every position greater than the index and within the list's bounds, either the element at that position is null and was null before, or it remains unchanged from its previous state."

Interpretation: If index valid (0 <= index < size()) and role != null, then for all positions i such that i > index && i < size() (list's bounds after operation? The operation does not change size, just sets element at index). So after set, for each i > index && i < size(), either this.get(i) == null and \old(this.get(i)) == null, or this.get(i) equals \old(this.get(i)). Essentially element unchanged unless null? Actually condition: either element at that position is null and was null before, or it remains unchanged from its previous state. That is equivalent to: this.get(i) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). But simpler: this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)) || (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null). But equality of objects: we can use == for null check, and .equals for non-null? Since Role type, we can use equals. We can express: (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). But need to ensure not null before calling equals; we can use (this.get(i) != null ? this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) : \old(this.get(i)) == null). However spec may accept simpler: this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)). Since if both null, equality holds. In Java, == on objects checks reference equality, not .equals. But for null it's fine; for non-null, reference equality may not hold even if equal. The comment says "remains unchanged from its previous state" meaning same object reference? Probably yes, unchanged element means same reference. So we can assert this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)). And also includes case both null. So we can assert this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i)). That's enough.

Thus assert: (0 <= index && index < this.size() && role != null) => \forall int i; i > index && i < this.size(); this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i));

Need to capture size after operation; size unchanged. Use this.size().

Thus final assert statement:

assert (0 <= index && index < this.size() && role != null) => \forall int i; i > index && i < this.size(); this.get(i) == \old(this.get(i));

